1
00:00:00,070 --> 00:00:04,210
London, 1696. This man could be hanged for

2
00:00:04,210 --> 00:00:05,650
what he's doing with these scissors.

3
00:00:05,970 --> 00:00:07,790
He snips the edge off a silver coin.

4
00:00:08,250 --> 00:00:08,950
Just a sliver.

5
00:00:09,190 --> 00:00:09,910
Then the next coin.

6
00:00:10,290 --> 00:00:10,990
And the next.

7
00:00:11,270 --> 00:00:13,250
He melts the silver slivers into a lump,

8
00:00:13,250 --> 00:00:15,470
and spends the shaved coins as if nothing

9
00:00:15,470 --> 00:00:16,170
happened.

10
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:18,420
All over England, people are doing this.

11
00:00:18,720 --> 00:00:20,100
And the man sent to stop them...

12
00:00:20,100 --> 00:00:21,890
is Isaac Newton.

13
00:00:22,190 --> 00:00:23,750
His answer is still in your pocket today.

14
00:00:24,370 --> 00:00:26,530
So is the answer to stop signs, manholes,

15
00:00:26,530 --> 00:00:27,920
bottle caps and crackers.

16
00:00:28,220 --> 00:00:30,140
Because almost every strange shape around

17
00:00:30,140 --> 00:00:32,460
you was a fix for problems somebody had.

18
00:00:34,130 --> 00:00:36,470
So why do so many coins have tiny

19
00:00:36,470 --> 00:00:37,720
ridges on the edge?

20
00:00:38,320 --> 00:00:40,260
Most coins in English pockets were old

21
00:00:40,260 --> 00:00:41,700
ones, hammered out by hand.

22
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:44,200
They were never perfectly round, so nobody

23
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:45,650
noticed a missing edge.

24
00:00:45,950 --> 00:00:47,870
Clipping was so serious it counted as

25
00:00:47,870 --> 00:00:50,040
treason. You could be hanged for it.

26
00:00:50,340 --> 00:00:52,940
In 1696, England called in the old

27
00:00:52,940 --> 00:00:54,860
hammered coins and replaced them with

28
00:00:54,860 --> 00:00:57,190
machine-made ones, with patterned edges.

29
00:00:57,510 --> 00:00:59,190
Now, if someone trimmed a coin, the

30
00:00:59,190 --> 00:01:00,150
pattern was gone.

31
00:01:00,730 --> 00:01:02,050
Anyone could see it in a second.

32
00:01:02,350 --> 00:01:04,170
Newton ran the Mint's hunt for coin

33
00:01:04,170 --> 00:01:06,010
criminals. He collected the evidence

34
00:01:06,010 --> 00:01:07,510
against one of London's most famous

35
00:01:07,510 --> 00:01:09,260
forgers, William Chaloner.

36
00:01:09,570 --> 00:01:12,310
In 1699, Chaloner was hanged.

37
00:01:12,670 --> 00:01:14,990
Today, an American dime or quarter has no

38
00:01:14,990 --> 00:01:16,050
silver in it at all.

39
00:01:16,330 --> 00:01:17,500
There's nothing worth shaving.

40
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:18,820
So why keep the ridges?

41
00:01:19,500 --> 00:01:21,460
Partly tradition... and partly your

42
00:01:21,460 --> 00:01:23,520
fingers. A dime and a penny are almost

43
00:01:23,520 --> 00:01:24,220
the same size.

44
00:01:24,660 --> 00:01:26,620
With your eyes closed, the ridges tell you

45
00:01:26,620 --> 00:01:27,300
which is which.

46
00:01:28,970 --> 00:01:30,290
Now look down.

47
00:01:30,970 --> 00:01:33,160
Why is almost every manhole cover round?

48
00:01:33,650 --> 00:01:34,710
Try this with a square one.

49
00:01:35,230 --> 00:01:36,830
Turn it on its side, and the long

50
00:01:36,830 --> 00:01:38,630
diagonal fits right through the hole.

51
00:01:39,080 --> 00:01:41,500
Clang. A heavy iron lid falls into the

52
00:01:41,500 --> 00:01:43,670
sewer... maybe onto a worker.

53
00:01:43,970 --> 00:01:46,210
A circle is the same width in every

54
00:01:46,210 --> 00:01:48,490
direction. However you turn it, it can

55
00:01:48,490 --> 00:01:49,900
never fall through its own hole.

56
00:01:50,230 --> 00:01:51,690
A round lid is also easy to roll,

57
00:01:51,830 --> 00:01:52,750
instead of lifting it.

58
00:01:53,290 --> 00:01:55,570
And it drops back in at any angle.

59
00:01:55,870 --> 00:01:58,050
A rounded triangle, called a Reuleaux

60
00:01:58,050 --> 00:01:59,690
triangle, is the same width in every

61
00:01:59,690 --> 00:02:00,500
direction too.

62
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:02,440
Engineers just found the circle easier to

63
00:02:02,440 --> 00:02:02,800
make.

64
00:02:04,470 --> 00:02:06,030
Now, the stop sign.

65
00:02:06,730 --> 00:02:08,870
Why does it have eight sides?

66
00:02:09,360 --> 00:02:12,180
In 1923, a group of American road

67
00:02:12,180 --> 00:02:14,960
engineers had a strange idea: the more

68
00:02:14,960 --> 00:02:17,620
sides a sign has, the more dangerous the

69
00:02:17,620 --> 00:02:18,320
place.

70
00:02:18,490 --> 00:02:20,770
A circle, with endless sides, meant a

71
00:02:20,770 --> 00:02:22,910
railway crossing: the deadliest spot of

72
00:02:22,910 --> 00:02:23,610
all.

73
00:02:23,690 --> 00:02:25,110
Eight sides meant stop.

74
00:02:25,770 --> 00:02:26,930
A diamond meant caution.

75
00:02:27,350 --> 00:02:28,570
A square meant care.

76
00:02:29,030 --> 00:02:30,640
A rectangle was just information.

77
00:02:30,960 --> 00:02:31,800
And here's the twist.

78
00:02:32,300 --> 00:02:34,500
For about thirty years, most American stop

79
00:02:34,500 --> 00:02:35,910
signs weren't red.

80
00:02:36,230 --> 00:02:38,220
They were yellow, with black letters.

81
00:02:38,600 --> 00:02:39,820
Red paint faded in the sun.

82
00:02:40,140 --> 00:02:41,860
A pale pink sign is easy to miss,

83
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:43,330
and that's dangerous.

84
00:02:43,650 --> 00:02:46,230
Only in 1954, when paint and reflective

85
00:02:46,230 --> 00:02:48,670
material lasted, did the stop sign finally

86
00:02:48,670 --> 00:02:49,370
turn red.

87
00:02:51,040 --> 00:02:52,480
Next, the grocery bag.

88
00:02:53,220 --> 00:02:54,940
Why does a paper bag have a flat,

89
00:02:54,940 --> 00:02:55,790
square bottom?

90
00:02:56,280 --> 00:02:58,300
Early paper bags were like big envelopes.

91
00:02:58,920 --> 00:03:00,640
Flat, pointed at the bottom...

92
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:01,760
and they fell over.

93
00:03:02,080 --> 00:03:04,360
In the late 1860s, Margaret Knight worked

94
00:03:04,360 --> 00:03:06,650
in a paper bag factory in Massachusetts.

95
00:03:07,030 --> 00:03:08,290
She built a wooden model of a machine

96
00:03:08,290 --> 00:03:09,970
that folded and glued a bag with a

97
00:03:09,970 --> 00:03:10,880
flat bottom.

98
00:03:11,180 --> 00:03:12,340
She took it to a machine shop to

99
00:03:12,340 --> 00:03:13,160
have it made in iron.

100
00:03:13,820 --> 00:03:16,640
And a man named Charles Annan saw it...

101
00:03:16,640 --> 00:03:18,090
and patented it first.

102
00:03:18,390 --> 00:03:20,750
In court, Annan's argument was simple: a

103
00:03:20,750 --> 00:03:22,510
woman couldn't possibly design a machine

104
00:03:22,510 --> 00:03:23,210
like this.

105
00:03:23,510 --> 00:03:25,460
Knight brought her drawings, her notes and

106
00:03:25,460 --> 00:03:26,210
her diary.

107
00:03:26,550 --> 00:03:28,490
In 1871, she won.

108
00:03:29,050 --> 00:03:30,010
The patent was hers.

109
00:03:30,630 --> 00:03:31,790
And the bag that stands up on its

110
00:03:31,790 --> 00:03:33,250
own is still her design.

111
00:03:34,920 --> 00:03:36,640
Now count the teeth on a bottle cap.

112
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:39,190
Twenty-one. Almost every time.

113
00:03:39,540 --> 00:03:41,860
In the 1880s, soda and beer bottles were

114
00:03:41,860 --> 00:03:43,180
sealed with corks and wire.

115
00:03:43,620 --> 00:03:45,520
The gas leaked, and the drink went flat.

116
00:03:45,860 --> 00:03:48,440
In 1892, in Baltimore, William Painter

117
00:03:48,440 --> 00:03:50,680
patented a metal cap with a crimped skirt,

118
00:03:51,080 --> 00:03:52,030
like a little crown.

119
00:03:52,340 --> 00:03:54,140
His first caps had twenty-four teeth.

120
00:03:54,440 --> 00:03:56,100
But over the years, makers settled on

121
00:03:56,100 --> 00:03:58,300
twenty-one. Enough to hold the fizz in,

122
00:03:58,300 --> 00:03:59,860
and still easy to pop off with an

123
00:03:59,860 --> 00:04:00,560
opener.

124
00:04:00,590 --> 00:04:02,150
Painter even sold the bottle opener.

125
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:04,500
And one of his salesmen got some famous

126
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:07,160
advice. Painter told him: invent something

127
00:04:07,160 --> 00:04:09,800
that people use once, throw away, and buy

128
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:10,500
again.

129
00:04:10,650 --> 00:04:12,050
That salesman was King Gillette.

130
00:04:12,670 --> 00:04:14,250
A few years later, he had the idea

131
00:04:14,250 --> 00:04:15,770
for the throwaway razor blade.

132
00:04:17,440 --> 00:04:19,740
Next, something hiding behind your sofa.

133
00:04:20,600 --> 00:04:22,380
Why do the flat prongs on an American

134
00:04:22,380 --> 00:04:24,030
plug have little holes?

135
00:04:24,510 --> 00:04:25,790
The first answer was clever.

136
00:04:26,330 --> 00:04:28,610
Some early sockets had tiny bumps inside

137
00:04:28,610 --> 00:04:30,670
that clicked into the holes, so the plug

138
00:04:30,670 --> 00:04:31,580
wouldn't slip out.

139
00:04:31,880 --> 00:04:34,140
But open most sockets today, and there's

140
00:04:34,140 --> 00:04:35,040
no bump at all.

141
00:04:35,620 --> 00:04:37,320
The prongs are just gripped by springy

142
00:04:37,320 --> 00:04:37,970
metal.

143
00:04:37,970 --> 00:04:39,370
So what are the holes for now?

144
00:04:40,250 --> 00:04:42,490
The official standard answers in one dry

145
00:04:42,490 --> 00:04:45,030
line: the hole is optional, and it's for

146
00:04:45,030 --> 00:04:45,890
manufacturing.

147
00:04:46,190 --> 00:04:48,350
The holes help hold the metal prongs in

148
00:04:48,350 --> 00:04:50,430
place while the plastic plug is moulded

149
00:04:50,430 --> 00:04:51,130
around them.

150
00:04:51,460 --> 00:04:53,500
Sometimes the strangest shape has the most

151
00:04:53,500 --> 00:04:54,240
boring answer.

152
00:04:55,910 --> 00:04:58,110
Last one. It's on your plate.

153
00:04:59,090 --> 00:05:00,850
Why do crackers have little holes?

154
00:05:01,330 --> 00:05:02,310
It isn't decoration.

155
00:05:03,010 --> 00:05:05,390
When cracker dough bakes, the water inside

156
00:05:05,390 --> 00:05:06,470
turns to steam.

157
00:05:06,770 --> 00:05:08,670
With nowhere to go, the steam blows

158
00:05:08,670 --> 00:05:11,150
bubbles, and the cracker puffs up like a

159
00:05:11,150 --> 00:05:11,850
pillow.

160
00:05:12,130 --> 00:05:14,470
So bakers poke holes into the dough before

161
00:05:14,470 --> 00:05:15,470
it goes into the oven.

162
00:05:15,850 --> 00:05:16,810
It's called docking.

163
00:05:17,110 --> 00:05:18,910
The steam escapes through the holes, and

164
00:05:18,910 --> 00:05:21,190
the cracker stays flat and crisp.

165
00:05:21,490 --> 00:05:22,870
Too few holes, and it puffs.

166
00:05:23,250 --> 00:05:25,390
Too many, and it turns hard and dry.

167
00:05:26,030 --> 00:05:28,370
The pattern is measured, not random.

168
00:05:30,040 --> 00:05:31,500
Ridges that catch thieves.

169
00:05:32,020 --> 00:05:33,280
A lid that can't fall in.

170
00:05:33,680 --> 00:05:35,200
A colour that waited thirty years.

171
00:05:35,680 --> 00:05:37,160
A bag that won in court.

172
00:05:37,500 --> 00:05:39,160
A cap that started a razor company.

173
00:05:39,720 --> 00:05:41,670
And holes that let a cracker breathe.

174
00:05:42,110 --> 00:05:43,680
None of these shapes are an accident.

175
00:05:44,160 --> 00:05:45,680
So next time you pick up a coin,

176
00:05:46,040 --> 00:05:47,320
run your thumb along the edge.

177
00:05:47,800 --> 00:05:49,580
You're touching the trap Newton set for

178
00:05:49,580 --> 00:05:50,280
thieves.

179
00:05:50,520 --> 00:05:52,180
Which shape around you should we explain

180
00:05:52,180 --> 00:05:54,020
next? Tell us in the comments.
